@


\section{Default arguments for matching courses}

The first argument we need is to identify a set of courses.
For this we might also have to identify a set of registers.
We'll use the argument and option defined in the [[cli.courses]] module.
We'll default to all courses ([[.*]]) in the register of the [[mine]] command.
<<argument for matching courses>>=
course: Annotated[str, coursescli.course_arg_regex]
<<option for matching registers, default to mine>>=
register: Annotated[str, coursescli.register_opt_regex] = coursescli.MINE
@

Then we can create a list of course--configuration pairs.
We delegate the iteration and error handling to
[[coursescli.get_courses_configs]], which centralises the logic shared
with [[schedule]] and [[hr]].
If the result is empty we exit with a non-zero return code, since every
signupsheets subcommand requires at least one course.
<<set list [[courses]] to ((course, register), config)-pairs>>=
courses = coursescli.get_courses_configs(course, register)
if not courses:
  sys.exit(1)
@

The same course lookup and sign-up-sheet reading logic is also useful
for shell completion.  Both [[schedule]] and [[hr]] need to suggest TA
usernames from the matching courses' sheets, so we keep that completer
here with the sign-up-sheet code instead of duplicating it in each
command module.

Like the schedule completer it replaces, this helper may need to fetch a
remote CSV when the sign-up sheet lives in Google Sheets or similar.
That makes failures normal rather than exceptional at completion time:
the shell asked for suggestions, but the command itself has not started
yet.  We therefore swallow per-course failures and return whatever names
we managed to gather, which keeps \textsc{tab} completion responsive.

Completion is also why [[sheets]] is imported inside the function:
every \textsc{tab} press starts a fresh interpreter that imports all
CLI modules, so a top-level import here would slow down completing
\emph{unrelated} commands too.
Inside the function, only the completions that actually read a
sign-up sheet pay for the import.
<<helper functions>>=
def complete_signup_users(ctx, args, incomplete):
  """Complete usernames from matching courses' sign-up sheets."""
  from nytid.signup import sheets
  try:
    course = course_in_effect(ctx) or ".*"
    register = ctx.params.get("register") or coursescli.MINE
    users = set()
    for (_, _), config in coursescli.get_courses_configs(
        course, register).items():
      try:
        url = config.get(SIGNUPSHEET_URL_PATH)
        if "docs.google.com" in url:
          url = sheets.google_sheet_to_csv_url(url)
        for row in sheets.read_signup_sheet_from_url(url):
          users.update(sheets.get_TAs_from_csv(row))
      except Exception:
        continue
    return sorted(u for u in users if u.startswith(incomplete))
  except Exception:
    return []
@

The course takes some finding, because the parsed values in [[ctx.params]] are
not enough.
When we complete [[--user]], the command line ends in an option that is still
waiting for its value.
To the parser that is a usage error, and it stops before it has handed out the
positional arguments.
During completion the error is swallowed, so we get a context where the course
is still at its default and the word we typed lies among the leftovers in
[[ctx.args]].
Those leftovers are in the order they were typed, so pairing them with the
command's arguments in the order they are declared tells us which is which.
That pairing is why we can't simply take the first leftover: in
[[hr amanuensis view]], the only positional argument is the [[user_regex]].

When the parser does reach the end, as when we complete a positional
[[user_regex]], the course is in [[ctx.params]] and normally nothing is left
over.
The exception is a surplus word, which we would then take for the course.
The command would reject that line anyway, so the worst that can happen is a
wrong suggestion, and we don't add code to tell the two cases apart.
A leftover must win over [[ctx.params]], since a default like [[.*]] looks just
like a course that was given.
We also look for both names, since [[hr]] calls the argument [[course_regex]]
and the other modules call it [[course]].
<<helper functions>>=
def course_in_effect(ctx):
  """Returns the given or default course regex, None if there is neither."""
  arguments = [param.name for param in ctx.command.params
               if param.param_type_name == "argument"]
  leftovers = dict(zip(arguments, ctx.args))
  for name in ("course", "course_regex"):
    course = leftovers.get(name) or ctx.params.get(name)
    if course:
      return course
  return None
@

We can verify the gathering and prefix filtering without touching the
network by stubbing the course lookup on the [[signupsheets]] module
and the sheet-reading functions on [[nytid.signup.sheets]] itself.
Patching the source module is what the deferred import requires:
[[sheets]] is no longer an attribute of the [[signupsheets]] module,
but the function-local [[from nytid.signup import sheets]] binds the
same module object, so an attribute patched there is seen at call
time.
An empty context exercises the default course and register fallback
used by commands that only know the current user's courses.

<<test functions>>=
def test_complete_signup_users(monkeypatch):
  """Completion gathers TA usernames from sign-up sheets."""
  config = MagicMock()
  config.get.return_value = "http://example/sheet.csv"
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    signupsheets_module.coursescli, "get_courses_configs",
    lambda course, register: {("c", "r"): config},
  )
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    nytid.signup.sheets, "read_signup_sheet_from_url",
    lambda url: [["row1"], ["row2"]],
  )
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    nytid.signup.sheets, "get_TAs_from_csv",
    lambda row: ["alice", "bob"] if row == ["row1"]
                else ["carol"],
  )
  ctx = MagicMock()
  ctx.params = {}
  assert complete_signup_users(ctx, None, "") == [
    "alice", "bob", "carol",
  ]
  assert complete_signup_users(ctx, None, "a") == ["alice"]
  assert complete_signup_users(ctx, None, "z") == []
@

That test hands the completer a context we built ourselves, so it can only
confirm what we believe a context looks like.
What the completer really gets depends on how the argument is named and how far
the parser came, so the next tests ask the way a shell does: they set the
variables of the completion protocol and run the whole [[nytid]] command tree.
The register, the courses and their configuration are real as well.
Only the sheet's download is replaced, since it goes over the network, and its
rows keep the real layout so that [[get_TAs_from_csv]] still reads them.
<<test functions>>=
REGISTER = "completionregister"

@pytest.fixture
def courses_with_sheets(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  """Two courses in a real register, each with one TA on its sheet."""
  runner = CliRunner()
  TAs = {"prgi": "tolvan", "prgm": "olvan"}
  columns = len(nytid.signup.sheets.SIGNUP_SHEET_HEADER)
  runner.invoke(nytid_cli, ["courses", "registry", "add",
                            REGISTER, str(tmp_path)])
  for course in TAs:
    runner.invoke(nytid_cli, ["courses", "new", course, "--register", REGISTER])
    runner.invoke(nytid_cli, ["signupsheets", "set-url", course,
                              f"http://example/{course}",
                              "--register", REGISTER])
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    nytid.signup.sheets, "read_signup_sheet_from_url",
    lambda url: [[""] * columns + [TAs[url.rpartition("/")[2]]]],
  )
  yield
  runner.invoke(nytid_cli, ["courses", "registry", "rm", REGISTER])

def complete(*words, incomplete=""):
  """What the shell would offer after typing `words` and pressing TAB."""
  line = ["nytid", *words, incomplete]
  result = CliRunner().invoke(nytid_cli, [], prog_name="nytid", env={
    "_NYTID_COMPLETE": "complete_bash",
    "COMP_WORDS": " ".join(line),
    "COMP_CWORD": str(len(line) - 1),
  })
  assert result.exit_code == 0
  return result.stdout.split()
@

Every command that offers these names must keep to the course we gave.
The list covers both spellings of the argument, and both ways the parser can
end: stopped by an option waiting for its value, and reaching the end when we
complete a positional [[user_regex]].
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("command", [
  ["hr", "timesheets", "generate", "prgi", "--register", REGISTER, "--user"],
  ["hr", "timesheets", "generate", "--register", REGISTER, "prgi", "--user"],
  ["hr", "amanuensis", "create", "prgi", "--register", REGISTER, "--user"],
  ["hr", "medvind", "deviations", "prgi", "--register", REGISTER, "--user"],
  ["hr", "medvind", "deviations", "prgi", "--register", REGISTER, "-u"],
  ["hr", "employed", "missing", "prgi", "--register", REGISTER],
  ["schedule", "view", "prgi", "--register", REGISTER, "--user"],
])
def test_completing_users_keeps_to_the_course(courses_with_sheets, command):
  assert complete(*command) == ["tolvan"]
